## Step 2: Execute the Script and Analyze Logs
Run the tracking program through your Node runtime terminal profile:

node monitored_field_detector.js

As it iterates, it will generate dedicated .txt files for every payload execution pass. You can run a terminal review to inspect how the target platform's validation filtering altered your strings:

# View one of the saved transaction logs to see the server behavior data
cat log_Username_Field_*.txt

## How to use this log data for your Bug Bounty Report:

* Look for error logs in Stream 1: If the server prints back a SQL array runtime trace or a generic "Server Error 500" code block inside lastServerResponseText, you have solid indicator data for an injection flaw.
* Look for raw strings in Stream 2: If visibleHtmlTree shows your text unaltered (e.g., <script>alert(1)</script>) rather than escaped (&lt;script&gt;), but it didn't fire an alert box natively, you can examine why the execution failed to craft a better payload layout.

